# LoRaMINT API - example configuration.
# Copy to .env and fill in the values. Every variable the application reads is
# listed here; the commented-out ones are optional and show their default.

#====================================
# Core
#====================================

# Postgres connection, read by bun's built-in `sql` client. Required.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://loramint:loramint@localhost:5432/loramint

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of this server. Only the login throttle
# uses it, and only to tell one visitor from another: behind Traefik every
# request arrives from Traefik, so with 0 the throttle would count all visitors
# as one and six wrong passwords anywhere would lock the whole site out.
#
#   0  no proxy - use the connection's own address and ignore X-Forwarded-For.
#      Correct for a server reachable directly, where that header is simply
#      whatever the client felt like writing.
#   1  the default, and what compose.prod.yml sets up: Traefik and nothing else.
#   2  a CDN in front of Traefik, and so on - one per hop.
#
# Only safe while the container cannot be reached around the proxy.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=1

#====================================
# The restricted database roles
#====================================

# There is nothing to configure here any more, and that is the point.
#
# The application no longer queries through DATABASE_URL. That role owns the
# schema and is a superuser in the default Docker setup, so a flaw in any read
# path was worth `pg_read_file()`, the password hashes in `pg_authid` and shell
# commands through `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` - a shell on the database host rather
# than a few rows too many. It is now used by the migrations and by the role
# setup, and by nothing else.
#
# Four roles carry the running application, each holding only what its work
# needs. The connection follows the *intent of the operation*, not the module it
# is written in and not the role of whoever is signed in - an administrator who
# only reads on a page reads through the read-only role like everybody else:
#
#   loramint_readonly   every read the application makes, and the /sql page for
#                       everyone below admin. SELECT only, and the role itself
#                       carries default_transaction_read_only.
#   loramint_ingest     the TTN webhook. INSERT on measurements and log_entries
#                       and not even SELECT - it is the only externally
#                       reachable route that writes, and it reads nothing.
#   loramint_manage     every change a person makes. SELECT and INSERT on the
#                       two logs and nothing more, which is what makes the change
#                       log impossible to rewrite from the interface.
#   loramint_admin_sql  the /sql page for administrators.
#
# Their passwords are derived from the one in DATABASE_URL - see
# packages/api/lib/db-roles.ts. scripts/ensure-roles.ts and the application
# compute the same value independently, so no password has to be configured,
# passed or stored anywhere. Change the owner's and they all change with it; the
# entrypoint runs ensure-roles before the server and resets them.
#
# Whether the SQL page exists at all, and whether data may be edited, are now
# settings rather than the presence of a variable - switchable under
# Verwaltung > Konfiguration.

# How many rows a result may show, on both pages. Default: 200
#
# The cap is applied inside the database, so raising it costs page weight and
# rendering time, not database work. Measured on this machine with a three-column
# result: 200 rows are 48 KB, 1000 are 183 KB, 5000 are 863 KB, 20000 are 3.4 MB.
# Above roughly 2000 the page stops being something you read and becomes
# something you scroll - use the CSV export for that.
# QUERY_MAX_ROWS=200

# After how many milliseconds a statement is cancelled. Default: 5000
# The database roles carry their own limit as well, so this can only tighten it.
# QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Shared secret for the TTN webhook: TTN sends it as the X-Downlink-Apikey
# header and requests that do not match are rejected. Required.
TTN_APP_KEY=your-ttn-api-key

# HTTP port. Default: 8090
PORT=8090

# "development" enables the SSR dev overlay and drops the Secure flag from the
# session cookie so it works over plain http. Anything else, including unset,
# is treated as production.
# NODE_ENV=development

#====================================
# Data management (optional)
#====================================

# How many rows one block of a deletion removes. Default: 10000
# Not a ceiling: a deletion by filter takes as many blocks as it needs, with a
# progress page in between. The size is what keeps one block short, because a
# statement running for minutes holds locks on the table the webhook is
# inserting into - between the blocks they are free. A selection is still
# refused above this, but a selection is one page and never comes close.
# MANAGE_MAX_DELETE=10000

# After how many milliseconds a management statement is cancelled.
# Default: 30000. Longer than QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS on purpose - deleting a whole
# faulty measurement series legitimately takes a while.
# MANAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

#====================================
# Setup account (optional)
#====================================

# A local administrator beside the directory, for getting in before there is one.
# Without it there is no login without LDAP, and therefore no way to reach the
# pages on which LDAP itself gets configured.
#
# Meant for ONE person doing that job - it does not replace the directory.
# Everyone else signs in through LDAP as before and gets their roles from their
# groups, and the change log keeps showing real people's names.
#
# It is checked BEFORE the directory. A username that matches is decided locally
# and only locally: a wrong password is refused rather than passed on, so the
# name belongs unambiguously to this account.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=setup

# The password, in one of two forms. Prefer the hash: whoever reads this
# environment - through `docker inspect`, the container platform's interface or
# a copy of this file - then holds a hash and not a way in. Generate one with
#     bun scripts/hash-password.ts
# which asks for the password without echoing it, so it reaches neither the
# shell history nor the process list.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=$argon2id$v=19$m=65536,t=2,p=1$...

# The same in clear text. Allowed for convenience; the server says so in the log
# on every start. Ignored when ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is set.
# ADMIN_PW=ein-langes-passwort

#====================================
# Device management (optional, TTN)
#====================================

# Without both TTN_API_KEY and TTN_APPLICATION_ID the device page stays the
# announcement it has always been, and no request ever leaves this server for
# The Things Network.
#
# NOT the same thing as TTN_APP_KEY above, however alike the names look:
#   TTN_APP_KEY   a word the webhook sends us, which we compare. A shared secret.
#   TTN_API_KEY   a credential that may register devices in the application and
#                 read their root keys. A key to the front door.
# Setting them to one value means whoever can read the webhook configuration in
# TTN can also administer the application, so the server refuses to start if they
# match.
#
# Create it in the TTN console under the application > API keys, with these
# rights: view/edit end devices, and view/edit their keys. The last one is needed
# because an OTAA device is registered together with its AppKey, and because the
# detail page can show it again to administrators.
# TTN_API_KEY=NNSXS....
# TTN_APPLICATION_ID=loramint

# The cluster the application lives in.
# Default: https://eu1.cloud.thethings.network
# TTN_URL=https://eu1.cloud.thethings.network

# How long to wait for The Things Network. Default: 5000
# TTN_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# What every device registered from here gets. Properties of the deployment
# rather than of the individual device - one region, one kind of module - so the
# form states them instead of asking. The defaults are what the devices
# registered by hand so far already use.
# TTN_FREQUENCY_PLAN=EU_863_870_TTN
# TTN_LORAWAN_VERSION=MAC_V1_0_3
# TTN_REGIONAL_PARAMETERS=PHY_V1_0_3_REV_A

#====================================
# Legal pages (optional)
#====================================

# Each page only exists, and only appears in the "Kontakt" nav dropdown, when
# its variable is set. Use \n for line breaks.
# LEGAL_IMPRESSUM=Musterschule\nMusterstraße 1\n12345 Musterstadt
# LEGAL_DATENSCHUTZ=Es werden keine personenbezogenen Daten gespeichert.

#====================================
# Login (optional, LDAP)
#====================================

# The login is opt-in: without LDAP_URL there is no /login route and no login
# button, and the site behaves exactly as before. Setting it makes
# SESSION_SECRET and one of the two bind strategies below mandatory - the
# server then refuses to start rather than failing at every sign-in attempt.
#
# For a directory to develop against, see
# packages/api/dev_scripts/ldap/README.md - a throwaway OpenLDAP container with
# test users, ready-made settings for both strategies below, and a group the
# search filter can restrict on.

# Directory URL. Use ldaps:// in production; a plain ldap:// bind sends the
# password across the network in the clear.
# LDAP_URL=ldaps://ldap.example.org:636

# --- Strategy 1: direct bind ----------------------------------------------
# Builds the user's DN from the login name and binds with it. Needs no service
# account, but the DN has to be derivable from the login name.
# {username} is the only placeholder and is escaped per RFC 4514.
# LDAP_USER_DN_TEMPLATE=uid={username},ou=people,dc=example,dc=org

# --- Strategy 2: search and bind ------------------------------------------
# A service account looks the user up first, then the DN that was found is
# bound with the submitted password. Use this to allow logging in by mail
# address, or to restrict access to a group - the filter is where you say so.
# LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=service,ou=system,dc=example,dc=org
# LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=change-me
# LDAP_SEARCH_BASE=ou=people,dc=example,dc=org
# {username} is escaped per RFC 4515. Default: (uid={username})
# Quote it: bun reads the value either way, but the `&` makes an unquoted line
# a syntax error for `set -a; . .env`, which silently leaves the filter unset.
# LDAP_SEARCH_FILTER='(&(uid={username})(memberOf=cn=loramint,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=org))'
#
# Note on memberOf: Active Directory provides it out of the box, but in OpenLDAP
# it is an overlay that has to be loaded - without it the filter above silently
# matches nothing and every login fails. If the directory does not have it,
# restrict on an attribute of the user entry instead, for example
#   LDAP_SEARCH_FILTER='(&(uid={username})(employeeType=loramint))'
# A filter matching more than one entry is refused rather than guessed at.

# --- Optional tuning -------------------------------------------------------

# Attribute shown as the name in the header; falls back to the login name when
# the entry does not have it. Default: cn
# LDAP_DISPLAY_NAME_ATTRIBUTE=displayName

# --- Group membership ------------------------------------------------------
# Groups decide which queries a user may run on /sql (see services/catalog.ts).
# Without either option below users hold no groups, and only see the queries
# that are open to everyone.
#
# Groups are read once, at sign-in, and travel in the session cookie: removing
# someone from a group takes effect when their session expires, not instantly.
#
# Option A - an attribute on the user entry. Active Directory provides memberOf
# out of the box; OpenLDAP only with the memberof overlay loaded. Full DNs are
# reduced to the group name, so cn=lehrer,ou=groups,... becomes "lehrer".
# LDAP_GROUP_ATTRIBUTE=memberOf
#
# Option B - search the group entries for the ones listing this user. Works with
# plain groupOfNames and needs no overlay. {dn} is the user's DN, escaped.
# LDAP_GROUP_SEARCH_BASE=ou=groups,dc=example,dc=org
# Quote it, like LDAP_SEARCH_FILTER. Default: (member={dn})
# For posixGroup entries use '(memberUid={username})'.
# LDAP_GROUP_FILTER='(member={dn})'
# Attribute holding the group name. Default: cn
# LDAP_GROUP_NAME_ATTRIBUTE=cn

# --- Privileges ------------------------------------------------------------
# Four independent areas - none contains another, so whoever needs more than
# one is put in more than one group:
#
#   LDAP_DATA_GROUP        read-only: "Daten > SQL", plus editing and regrouping
#                          measurements.
#   LDAP_MANAGEMENT_GROUP  devices: register, rename, and assign to a group.
#                          No measurement editing.
#   LDAP_ADMIN_GROUP       everything, including configuration, data groups and
#                          the writable SQL console. Contains the other three.
#   LDAP_BOARD_GROUP       curates the public /board page: which measurements
#                          appear there and with what display range.
#
# The defaults are not symmetric, on purpose. Unset LDAP_DATA_GROUP means "no
# restriction configured", so every signed-in user keeps the read-only page and a
# deployment that never set up groups is not locked out. Unset
# LDAP_MANAGEMENT_GROUP, LDAP_ADMIN_GROUP or LDAP_BOARD_GROUP mean nobody reaches
# those areas: editing rights, write access and the board curation page are
# never handed out by accident.
# LDAP_DATA_GROUP=loramint
# LDAP_MANAGEMENT_GROUP=loramint-management
# LDAP_ADMIN_GROUP=loramint-admin
# LDAP_BOARD_GROUP=loramint-board

# The directory's own user administration - lldap's interface, a school portal.
# Passwords belong to the directory and not to this application, so the login
# form links there as "Passwort ändern in der Nutzerverwaltung".
#
# The label names the destination on purpose. Most such pages require signing in
# first, which is precisely what someone with a forgotten password cannot do;
# calling the link "Passwort vergessen?" would strand them. Set it only if the
# address is somewhere a signed-in user can actually change their password.
#
# Without it the form says to contact the administration.
# LDAP_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=https://portal.example.org/

# Set to false ONLY against a test directory with a self-signed certificate.
# Default: true
# LDAP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true

# Connect and response timeout in milliseconds. Default: 5000
# LDAP_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

#====================================
# Sessions (required once the login is enabled)
#====================================

# Signing key for the session cookie, at least 32 characters. Generate one with
#     openssl rand -base64 48
# The cookie is signed rather than encrypted, so this must stay out of version
# control. Rotating it invalidates every existing session - and since no
# sessions are stored server-side, that is also the only way to revoke them.
# SESSION_SECRET=

# How long a session stays valid, in hours. Default: 8
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=8
